WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:08.840
My name is Ariss.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.960
I am the co-owner of Awl Together Leather,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.590
and I have been a
professional leatherworker

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:13.730
for five years.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.730
Awl Together Leather is Canada's

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.530
first queer-owned cobbling shop.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.610
Today, I'm going to walk
you through how we restore

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.400
a faded and poorly redyed Dior handbag.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.790
This is our client's handbag.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.530
She brought it in because
she had previously

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.960
tried to redye it at a different shop

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.030
that did not strip the dye properly.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:31.971
When you look at this bag,

00:00:31.971 --> 00:00:34.546
you can see that it is gray.

00:00:34.546 --> 00:00:38.146
The handles are poorly warped and chipped.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.380
This Dior bag is leaking dye,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.650
and so we are going to have to deglaze it.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.610
To start, we will need to prep this bag

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.340
by taping off all portions

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.260
that we do not want to get dye on.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.220
To strip the bag,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.240
we will start by putting
on our safety equipment.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.910
Deglazer is a very strong chemical

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.150
that is formulated to remove sealant coats

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.353
and previous dye layers from leather.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.640
We will deglaze the bag
by going in small circles

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.306
with a medium-rough sponge

00:01:09.306 --> 00:01:10.672
to get the previous dye off

00:01:10.672 --> 00:01:13.200
and the previous sealant coat off.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.300
We need to evenly distribute the deglazer

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.500
over the bag to ensure that the dye

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.503
will evenly go back into
the bag after the fact.

00:01:23.393 --> 00:01:26.300
We are using a professional oil tan dye

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.360
formulated for leather.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.700
It is better than alcohol-based dyes

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.663
as it gives a more even coverage.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.005
We will be using a paintbrush

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:35.363
for all of the quilted areas

00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:36.660
to make sure we get the creases

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.210
and a wool dauber for the bottom,

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.743
where it is even leather.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.580
We are using a large
flat-bristled paintbrush

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.350
to make sure that our
strokes are wide and even,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.143
making for a uniform color.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.060
Interiors of bags pose challenges

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.430
because you are not able to dye liners.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.363
And so you have to make sure

00:02:00.363 --> 00:02:01.746
that you don't hit the liner,

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:04.538
which is why we'll protect it with tape.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.040
The handles' edges are too
badly cracked to be restored,

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.690
and the handles themselves
are bleeding so much dye

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.550
that we do not think we can successfully

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.130
prevent dye from bleeding
out of them in the future.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.330
We are going to remake them instead.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.911
To remove these handles,

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.161
I am going to pop these rivets

00:02:26.161 --> 00:02:29.383
using strong, sharp side
cutters and bolt cutters.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.440
To make the leather strap,

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.498
I am going to cut a piece of leather;

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.768
put it through my bell skiver,

00:02:47.768 --> 00:02:50.277
which is a machine that thins leather out;

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.136
glue that piece and wait for that to dry;

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.893
and then fold that piece over onto itself.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.300
I am going to use a seam
roller to fold these edges

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.280
to make sure that they become uniform

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.397
and even in a new strap.

00:03:20.039 --> 00:03:21.070
To remake these handles,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.490
I will be using an
Italian calfskin leather

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.760
in the same grain as the original bag.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.396
I am going to use the old handle

00:03:29.396 --> 00:03:31.371
as a stencil on my leather

00:03:31.371 --> 00:03:33.250
to make sure the shape stays the same.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.590
I am going to trace
the base of the handles

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.731
onto a leather,

00:03:37.731 --> 00:03:39.264
a leather board for structure,

00:03:39.264 --> 00:03:40.943
and a top layer of leather.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.710
I am then going to glue them all together,

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.523
making sure that no air
bubbles are in the glue.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.440
To make even, clean edges,

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.098
we are going to use an X-Acto knife

00:04:04.098 --> 00:04:05.481
and a corner punch.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.250
I'm going to sand the
edges of these handles

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.220
to make sure that they are perfectly even,

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.200
so that when I put on my edge coat

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.728
no burrs or bumps are shown.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.540
To put on the edge coat,
I am going to be using

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.410
a roller inkwell and a roller pen

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.735
to make sure it is evenly distributed.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.770
To place the handle to the strap,

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.410
I will be using some double-sided tape

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.252
to ensure it does not move while I sew it.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.150
I'm going to be sewing
this handle together

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.280
using an industrial walking foot machine,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.030
which evenly pulls the leather along

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.505
to make sure the stitch
lengths are all the same.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.170
Now I am going to apply
an acrylic leather sealer

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.620
to ensure that the dye

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.245
does not leak onto the customer

00:05:03.245 --> 00:05:05.295
and to also waterproof the bag.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.100
Now we are going to use a leather punch

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.800
and a leather rivet setter tool

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.407
to punch a hole into the handles

00:05:14.407 --> 00:05:17.207
and to put rivets through and set them.

00:05:25.742 --> 00:05:26.600
To finish the bag,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.083
I'm going to remove all the tape.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.960
And then buff it using
a true horsehide brush,

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:36.593
which will even out the shine

00:05:36.593 --> 00:05:38.218
and the gleam of the bag.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.253
Our customer was thrilled

00:05:46.253 --> 00:05:47.695
with the end result of this bag.

00:05:47.695 --> 00:05:49.442
She is extremely happy

00:05:49.442 --> 00:05:52.100
to have her handbag back in her wardrobe.

